<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/intermediate/news-vs-opinion-vs-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The news/opinion/analysis distinction is journalism's <strong>grammar of trust</strong>:
    each genre has a different relationship to evidence and to the reader — and keeping
    them legible is both the craft's discipline and the audience's defence. You know
    from the foundational lessons what news is <em>for</em> (a public that can hold power to
    account needs facts it can act on); this page is about how the industry sorts its
    output so the reader always knows which job a piece of writing is doing. The
    distinction matters because the three genres look superficially similar — same
    fonts, same site, same masthead — while making fundamentally different promises:
    news promises <em>this happened, checkably</em>; analysis promises <em>this is what it means,
    argued from evidence</em>; opinion promises <em>this is what we think, and you are entitled
    to disagree</em>. Break the promise and the reader cannot tell fact from advocacy —
    which is the exact condition misinformation exploits. This page covers what makes
    news (the news values and the reporting discipline behind them), how analysis earns
    its right to interpret, what opinion's honest form looks like, the labelling
    conventions that hold the system together, and why the lines blur in precisely the
    ways they do.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the verifiable, the explained, the argued, the label contract" widget="equationStep" config={genresCfg} />

  <h2>What makes news: finding and assessing stories</h2>
  <p>
    Before the genre lines can be drawn, the raw material — a story — has to be
    recognised. Editors assess potential stories against recurring <strong>news values</strong>:
    <em>impact</em> (how many people, how deeply — a change in the state pension age touches
    millions; a parking scheme, one town), <em>timeliness</em> (news decays; a story is "new"
    only while the reader has not heard it), <em>proximity</em> (physical and emotional —
    readers care about their street and about people like them), <em>prominence</em> (the
    same fire at a school and at a minister's house is not the same story),
    <em>conflict</em> and <em>controversy</em> (clashes of interest reveal stakes),
    <em>novelty</em> (the "man bites dog" asymmetry: deviations from expectation are
    informative precisely because they are rare), and <em>human interest</em>. Galtung and
    Ruge's classic 1963 study of foreign-news selection first systematised these; the
    working editor applies them in seconds, but the crucial discipline is that news
    values describe <em>attention</em>, not importance — a slow story (housing supply,
    pension futures) can matter more than a loud one, and beat journalism exists to
    catch what the values would filter out. Assessing a story also means assessing its
    <strong>evidence</strong>: who knows this, how do they know, can it be confirmed
    independently? That habit — verification before publication — is what the news
    genre is built from.
  </p>

  <h2>News: the verifiable claim</h2>
  <p>
    A news report's promise is <strong>factual: something happened, and here is how we know</strong>.
    Its grammar follows. Attribution is explicit — every significant claim is tied to a
    source ("police said", "according to the filing") — and the <em>kind</em> of attribution
    is part of the information: <strong>on the record</strong> (named and quotable),
    <strong>on background</strong> (usable, source described generically — "a senior official"),
    <strong>off the record</strong> (for the reporter's understanding, not publishable). The
    reporter's voice stays out of the sentence: verbs of happening, not of judging;
    adjectives that describe (72-metre, four-storey) rather than evaluate (shocking,
    humiliating); numbers instead of intensifiers ("a 40% fall", not "a dramatic slump").
    When judgement is genuinely in play, it is <em>attributed</em>: "opponents called the
    deal humiliating" reports the fact of the criticism without endorsing it. The
    classic test: every sentence of a news report should be checkable — a quote in a
    recording, a number in a document, an event with named witnesses. A sentence no
    one could check is either analysis or opinion, and it needs to move or be labelled.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "unbiased news" means the reporter has no perspective.
    Perspective is unavoidable — choosing which story to cover, which sources to call
    first and which fact leads the sentence are all editorial decisions. The honest
    discipline is not the impossible absence of viewpoint but <strong>method</strong>: verify
    before publishing, attribute what you were told, seek the other party's response,
    and keep your evaluations visibly labelled. Readers can audit a method; nobody can
    audit an invisible mind.</p>

  <h2>Analysis: the explained why</h2>
  <p>
    Analysis takes the news as its raw material and answers the question news cannot:
    <em>what does it mean?</em> Its tools are context (how does this compare with what
    happened before?), expertise (what do the rules, economics or chemistry imply?) and
    synthesis (which of these facts connect?). A <strong>worked example</strong> makes the
    genre's texture visible. Say the news is: "The Bank of England raised interest
    rates from 4.0% to 4.25%." The analysis piece then argues from evidence: what the
    last three rate decisions did to mortgage rates (the pass-through lag is typically
    months, not days), what the inflation report said about services prices (the Bank's
    stated focus), what this implies for the autumn forecast — and it reaches a
    defensible conclusion, e.g. "this marks the point where the committee's attention
    has shifted from bringing inflation down to protecting the currency's credibility."
    Notice what the analysis writer <em>may</em> do (hold a thesis, emphasise some facts,
    make a judgement call) and what they <em>may not</em> (distort or omit evidence,
    advocate, misstate the record) — an analysis piece that misquotes the report it
    interprets is a firing offence, exactly as a news story that invents a quote is.
    The genre's honest label matters as much as its content: outlets mark analysis
    explicitly ("Analysis" in the strapline) precisely because the interpretive voice
    is stronger than a news report's.
  </p>

  <h2>Opinion: the argued position</h2>
  <p>
    Opinion writing is the genre where advocacy is not just permitted but required:
    the columnist, the leader writer and the op-ed contributor argue for a position,
    using evidence <em>rhetorically</em> — selected, framed and deployed in service of the
    case. Its two house forms matter. The <strong>editorial</strong> ("leader") speaks for the
    institution: unsigned, reflecting the outlet's editorial line, which is why
    newspaper endorsements and leaders are treated as institutional positions rather
    than individual ones. The <strong>column</strong> and <strong>op-ed</strong> speak for a named
    person — the modern op-ed page dates from 1970, when the New York Times began
    running signed commentary literally <em>opposite the editorial page</em>, and the name
    stuck. The honest opinion piece still has rules: facts cited within it must be
    real (opinion libel law, the "fair comment" defence, protects argument about
    provable facts but not false statements of fact); counter-arguments should be
    represented well enough to be recognisable; and the reader must know whose voice
    they are hearing — hence bylines, biographies and conflict-of-interest
    disclosures. Opinion done honestly is a public good (it models argument; it
    surfaces positions); opinion dressed as news is the trust-destroying counterfeit
    this page exists to arm you against.
  </p>

  <h2>The labels are the contract — and the fourth and fifth genres</h2>
  <p>
    The genre system only works if the reader can tell, in seconds, which contract a
    piece is operating under. That is the job of <strong>labelling</strong>: news unmarked,
    analysis marked, opinion marked, satire obvious, and sponsorship announced. Two
    further genres test the system. <strong>Sponsored content</strong> (native advertising)
    is advertising wearing the newsroom's typography; regulators such as the UK's ASA
    and the US FTC require disclosure, and the professional standard — label it loudly,
    keep it visually distinct, never let the commercial desk touch news copy — exists
    because the borrowed credibility is the product being sold. <strong>Satire</strong> is
    fiction operating under a convention both sides know (The Onion's readers know the
    stories are invented); satire becomes misinformation only when the context is
    stripped — a screenshot travelling without its masthead. The general law of the
    label discipline: <em>the more a format resembles news, the more explicit its label
    must be</em>. On social platforms, where formats blur (60-second explainer videos,
    personal newsletters, a reporter's own account), the discipline travels with the
    journalist: say what you are doing — "here's my view", "here's what's confirmed" —
    inside the format itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the lines blur — and why the wall still pays</h2>
  <p>
    If the distinction is so valuable, why does it erode? Follow the incentives.
    <strong>Opinion travels.</strong> Engagement metrics reward provocation and certainty;
    measured hedging loses to confident advocacy in every recommendation algorithm,
    so the commercial pressure is always toward the opinion product, whichever label
    it wears. <strong>Analysis is expensive.</strong> It requires beat expertise and time;
    in a shrinking economics of news, the interpretive layer is the first squeezed.
    <strong>Blurring is profitable</strong> for the same reason counterfeit money exists:
    the credibility of the news genre is an asset that others can spend. Against this,
    the trust evidence runs the other way: the Reuters Institute's Digital News
    Report has tracked, year on year, that audiences selectively avoid news they
    perceive as biased, and trust surveys consistently rank clearly-separated,
    transparently-labelled journalism higher. Trust is the newsroom's only durable
    asset — slow to build, quick to spend, and impossible to buy back at the same
    price. The wall between news and opinion is not prudishness; it is the balance
    sheet.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading like an editor: the tells</h2>
  <p>
    The audience's defence is genre literacy, and it is trainable. <strong>Check the
    label first</strong> — straplines, section names, byline notes. Then audit the verbs
    and adjectives: news sentences describe and attribute; opinion sentences evaluate
    freely; analysis sentences interpret but stay anchored to citable evidence.
    Ask the audit questions: could I check this sentence? (news claim) — is there a
    thesis being argued from evidence? (analysis) — is a position being advocated?
    (opinion) — is something being sold? (sponsored) — is this invented on purpose?
    (satire). When a piece mixes genres, as long features often legitimately do
    (reportage with a concluding argument), the test is whether the <em>shifts are
    signposted</em> — the dishonest hybrid is the one that hides its argument inside
    its news.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The history is a pendulum.</strong> American nineteenth-century papers were
    openly partisan (party organs, not pretend-neutral); "objectivity" as a norm
    consolidated only in the 1920s–30s, partly as a response to propaganda after the
    First World War and the rise of the wire services, which needed to sell the same
    copy to papers of every politics. The BBC's impartiality rules and the US Fairness
    Doctrine (1949–1987) are institutional attempts to hold the line; cable news and
    the open web then re-fragmented the audience — the pendulum keeps swinging, which
    is why the <em>labels</em> matter more than the era's prevailing norm.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the boundaries are genuinely hard.</strong> Data journalism straddles the
    line (a methodology note is analysis; the chart is news); the "explainer" format
    smuggles judgement into news clothing; and the personal-newsletter economy puts
    one person in all three chairs in one send. The working answer is disclosure up
    front rather than purity of form. The live research question is measurement:
    outlets and academics are experimenting with machine-readable content labels
    (news vs opinion marked in the feed itself), because the strapline discipline
    evidently fails when content is consumed as a screenshot.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the verification lesson; Galtung &amp; Ruge, news-value study (1963); SPJ Code of Ethics; the genre-labelling policies of major outlets (Reuters, AP, NYT); Reuters Institute Digital News Report.</p>

